\f1\fs22 The green room was where the day had decided to keep him, and the day had its reasons. It was the festival\'92s backstage marquee, a canvas cathedral pitched behind the merch tents, and by dusk it had filled with the particular warmth of people being paid to enjoy themselves and succeeding beyond the brief. Chanta preferred the marquee. The marquee had a rider. A nine-piece with two drummers had come off stage and gone straight into a second, unpaid, better set in the corner by the eskies. Elvisettes moved through the guy-ropes in capes and glitter, and Chanta had spent the better part of the afternoon among them the way a stone spends an afternoon among ripples, at the centre of everything and going nowhere. Somebody kept handing him cocktails. They were the festival\'92s own, structurally cream and sugar and only theoretically alcohol, novelty things with umbrellas and names like weather, and he had been drinking them the way you drink water in a place where the water is dessert. Out past the canvas, up on the platform in front of the engineering tower, his throne sat empty. He knew it sat empty. He had chosen the distance himself, and chosen it precisely, and the choosing had not cost him one honest second of joy all day, which was its own kind of tax.\
