\f1\fs22 He was working out whether a Blue Hawaii counted as a fifth or as the tail of the fourth when the crowd behind him arranged itself, and the High Elvis was there. He came the way he always came, without hurry and without announcing that he wasn\'92t hurrying, in the suit that did none of the shouting, and he sat down on the esky beside Chanta as if they had a standing arrangement to. He didn\'92t reach for a drink. He watched the band a while, hands folded, a man giving the room its moment before he spent it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They fought all afternoon, out there,\'94 the High Elvis said at last, mild as a weather report. \'93Good-natured, mostly. Scored for degree of difficulty.\'94 He let that sit. \'93There would have been less fighting if there were more lyrics.\'94 He turned the folded hands over, examined them, put them back. \'93One song, Teacher. About the Dawkins \'97 the evolving, the whole beautiful accident of us. Something to set against the Rollers and their heaven. Something to close on.\'94 He said it the way a doctor names a dose, no bombast in it anywhere, only the flat pastoral certainty of a man who had spent the whole night talking people down off ledges and had one ledge left. \'93The night wants it. That\'92s all.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta looked at the umbrella in his drink. He wanted to say yes, and he wanted the yes to be true, and the trouble was the old ache, the one that had sat where it always sat for weeks now, low down and to the left \'97 the one that had Sarah\'92s name on it and, lately, a second smaller name folded inside the first. Because he could not write here. He knew he could not write here. He had proved it thirty instrumentals deep on a napkin in a studio, and the reason was simple and enormous and had a name, and the name was out on the platform in a velvet gown with an orange light inside her that only he had ever seen. His wellspring. His muse. Little Clinton, who gave him the songs, who had always given him the songs, and who was thirty-four weeks and a hundred metres away, riding out in front of Sarah like the figurehead of a ship he wasn\'92t allowed on. You couldn\'92t fetch water this far from the well. Anybody knew that. He wasn\'92t the brightest bulb in the amusement park, but the little he did know arrived with the confidence of scripture, and this he knew.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I need to be closer,\'94 he told the High Elvis, and stood, and meant the platform, and could not go to the platform, and so meant the next best thing. The tower. Up where the cables ran home, up alongside her, near enough the baby that the tap might turn. It was, he was certain, the only way the song would come \'97 up there, in range of the kid. He left the drink.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The tower was a working position, a scaffold stack of desks and rigging with the whole festival\'92s nervous system routed through it, and the two engineers up top had the settled, unbothered air of men whose headline act was somebody else\'92s problem tonight. They gave the Great Teacher a nod and went back to their faders. Chanta found a corner of railing at the high edge, alone with the dark and the drop, closer to Sarah\'92s throne than he had let himself be all day, and he waited for the baby to work.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The baby did not work. What came instead, unbidden, was the Bar. The Memphis Mafia Bar, where Lonnie used to pull his beer before he\'92d said hello, where a man could be nobody in peace \'97 and where he had walked, weeks back, into Rex Humble\'92s New Graceland, into the pressed shirts and the whitewash and the smell of somebody else\'92s salvation nailed up over the top of his own. He felt it the way you feel a tooth that\'92s gone: a specific absence with a shape. They\'92d done that. The Holy Rollers with their scrubbed faces and their heaven, they\'92d taken the one warm room in the Ghetto and hollowed it into a church, and they were hollowing the rest of it the same way, block by block, and the anger came up in him hot and clean and entirely his own, and had absolutely nothing to say for itself in the way of a chorus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The sun was going down among the fig trees. He watched it get low and molten and settle into the haze at the rim of the Bubble, a single sinking coal, and \'97 the way it always did, the way it had done on the ruined stairs the night he first found his voice \'97 the sun undid him. It swelled and softened and became the other thing, the orange glow, the supernatural warmth he had seen once through the wall of Sarah\'92s belly with Little Clinton curled inside it like a filament in a bulb. There it was. There the kid was. His whole heart tipped towards it across the hundred metres of dark. Thank you, he thought, thank you, here we go. He kissed two fingers and pressed them to the orange smudge of sun still burning on the horizon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And then Chanta left.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He would not, afterward, have called it dramatic. It was the least dramatic thing in the world. One moment he was slumped against the railing with a biro he did not remember picking up, and the next he had simply gone up out of himself, cheerful and weightless, the psychotropic warmth of the day\'92s cream cocktails carrying him off his own body the way a good current takes a man who has stopped bothering to swim. He looked back once and saw himself down there, folded over the desk, scribbling \'97 his hand was already scribbling, which struck him as tremendously good manners on his hand\'92s part \'97 and then he was flying, out and down over the lawn, and it was marvellous, and he thought, with the clear practical priority of a man having a religious experience, that he could murder a cheeseburger. Then he was on the platform. On his throne. The empty one, filled now, beside Sarah\'92s, close enough to touch the velvet, and she did not turn, and did not know, and the not-knowing was correct and complete; this was his to see and hers to be spared. Chanta never goes on. His body was up the tower with the biro. Only the seeing had come down.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the throne he could see what the throne saw, which was everything. Half a million of them out there in the dusk, faces up, going off like happy artillery \'97 and he understood, sitting in the seat they had built for him and crowned with melted bobbleheads, that he had had it only half right up the tower. It wasn\'92t just the Rollers. Look at them. Look at what they were doing to her, to the child, to him. The One After the One, they called the baby, and they meant it, they sang it, they knelt to a belly they couldn\'92t have named the owner of \'97 the whole worshipping ocean of them, the Elvi who loved him and the Cloudborne who loved the game, faces lifted to a mother\'92s face thirty feet high and worshipping the accident of her as hard as any Roller ever worshipped his heaven. Credulous. Every last happy one of them. Not a religion he could stand outside of and point at, but the whole air of the place, faith going up off the grass like heat. It wasn\'92t the Rollers he had to sing against. It was that. All of it. Every mind out there that had decided to kneel.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The words came then, and they came far too fast to catch, a flood with no bottom, and up the tower his hand was going like a thing possessed and the biro was digging the paper and he grabbed for more \'97 a festival pad off the sound desk, coffee-ringed, MONITOR MIX 3 biroed across the top \'97 and filled it. He did not read what he wrote. He never did. He knew where it came from; he always knew; he was writing down what the baby gave him, that was the whole arrangement, that was the muse doing at last what a muse was for. It was only that the lines coming out under his hand were about a gutted bar with someone else\'92s God nailed over the door, and about a lawn full of faces kneeling to nothing. He wrote until the flood ran dry and the sugar went out from under him all at once and left him shaking on the railing with a cramped hand and no cocktail, back in his body, gloriously, stupidly empty and whole.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He found his phone. He thumbed it, one word at a time, tongue in the corner of his mouth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>I have a song.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then he was up and gone, down the tower two rungs at a stretch with the notepad crushed to his chest \'97 not towards the platform, never the platform, not to the lights or the throne or the woman he still could not face \'97 but back the way he\'92d come, back towards the canvas and the eskies and the warm ordinary dark, running flat out for the green room. Thank you, Little Clinton, he thought, the whole way down. The kid had come through. The kid always came through.\
